title: The atom — Lubiatowo, the bill and the grid

@nuclear_contract_2024
# prose-audit: Grade A — The inherited Westinghouse contract is presented as a financing and ownership decision rather than a referendum on nuclear power, and the public-ownership, price-shield, supply-chain, grid-first and opposition routes each end in an authored political consequence.
title: The atom arrives as an invoice
subtitle: The previous government signed for three reactors at Lubiatowo; nobody has yet said who pays for them
new-page: true
tags: poland_event
priority: 25
order: 14
max-visits: 1
face-image: img/poland/events/lubiatowo-kopalino-2025.webp
view-if: continuous_campaign = 1 and nuclear_stage = 0 and ((year = 2024 and month >= 5) or year >= 2025)
on-arrival: {!
Q.nuclear_stage = 1;
Q.news_headline = "The first Polish nuclear plant becomes a question about money";
!}

= [+ date_label +] — Three reactors, one coastline, no financing model

**Polskie Elektrownie Jądrowe**, the wholly state-owned company, holds a
contract with the **Westinghouse–Bechtel** consortium for three AP1000 reactors
at **Lubiatowo-Kopalino**, in the commune of Choczewo on the Baltic coast. The
technology was chosen by resolution in 2022. The engineering contract was signed
in the autumn of 2023, weeks before the election, by a government that did not
have to explain how it would be paid for.

Now somebody does. The plant costs more than any Polish investment since the
war. The options are the ones every country with a nuclear programme has faced:
the state carries it, or the state shares it with capital that wants a return,
or the electricity bill carries it for thirty years.

[? if matysiak_nuclear_program >= 5: Inside our own coalition this is not a
fight between believers and opponents. **Marcelina Zawisza's** and **Paulina
Matysiak's** wing has argued for the atom for years, on the industrial and
climate case together, and expects the party to stop apologising for it.?][? if
matysiak_nuclear_program < 5: Our own people are split in the ordinary way:
the industrial and climate case on one side, the price of the capital and the
memory of Żarnowiec on the other.?]
[? if energy_security < 45: Coal still sets the price of Polish electricity and
the winter tariff argument reopens every autumn.?]
[? if left_in_government = 1: We are inside the cabinet that must answer, which
means our conditions are worth something exactly once.?][? if
left_in_government = 0: We are outside the cabinet, which means our conditions
are a public price list rather than a decision.?]

- @nuclear_public_ownership
- @nuclear_price_shield
- @nuclear_supply_chain
- @nuclear_grid_first
- @nuclear_opposition_backing

@nuclear_public_ownership
title: Write permanent public ownership into the programme
subtitle: One hundred per cent state-owned, no equity sale, and a statute that makes a later sell-off require a vote.
view-if: left_in_government = 1
on-arrival: {!
Q.nuclear_public_ownership += 25;
Q.nuclear_cost_pressure += 8;
Q.nuclear_left_credit += 5;
Q.nuclear_position = "Built and owned by the state, protected by statute";
Q.public_trust += 2;
Q.workers_support += 2;
Q.economic_issue_salience += 2;
Q.ko_relation -= 2;
Q.razem_dissent -= 3;
Q.last_policy_stage = "Implementation";
Q.last_policy_authority = "Council of Ministers ownership resolution and a companies bill";
Q.last_policy_implementation = "Public ownership of the nuclear programme fixed in statute";
Q.news_headline = "The nuclear programme is locked into public ownership";
!}

The company stays whole and the statute says so: no share of Polskie
Elektrownie Jądrowe may be transferred without an act of parliament. It costs
nothing today and forecloses the cheapest option available to every future
finance minister, which is precisely the point.

The Ministry of Finance's objection is written in the polite register officials
use when they intend to reopen a question in three years. Our answer, repeated
in every committee, is that a country which sold its power stations once already
knows what the second act looks like. The energy unions, who have watched two
decades of restructuring, treat the clause as the first thing this government
has done for them.

- @poland_event_queue: Fix the ownership clause

@nuclear_price_shield
title: Tie the programme to what households pay
subtitle: Cost: 1 resource. No public capital without a legal shield on the bills of the people financing it.
choose-if: resources >= 1
unavailable-subtitle: The party cannot cost and defend a tariff mechanism this month.
on-arrival: {!
Q.resources -= 1;
Q.nuclear_price_shield += 20;
Q.nuclear_left_credit += 6;
Q.nuclear_cost_pressure += 6;
Q.nuclear_position = "Public capital tied to a legal shield on household bills";
Q.social_spending_support += 2;
Q.public_trust += 3;
Q.pensioner_support += 2;
Q.workers_support += 2;
if (Q.left_in_government) {
    Q.household_security += 3;
    Q.last_policy_stage = "Implementation";
    Q.last_policy_authority = "Energy ministry regulation and the tariff regulator";
    Q.last_policy_implementation = "Household price shield attached to the nuclear financing model";
} else {
    Q.last_policy_stage = "Proposal";
    Q.last_policy_authority = "Opposition bill and public campaign";
    Q.last_policy_implementation = "Not enacted";
}
Q.news_headline = "Lewica ties the nuclear programme to a household price shield";
!}

The condition is one sentence long: if the public finances the plant, the public
does not pay twice. A regulated social tariff for low-income households, an
indexation formula that cannot be quietly rewritten, and a ban on passing
construction risk into the bill during the build.

It is the least glamorous form of energy politics and the only one most people
will ever notice. The industry calls the mechanism rigid. Our own answer is a
number: the share of Polish households that reduced heating last winter, which
is high enough that nobody in the room disputes it. [? if left_in_government =
0: From opposition the shield is a bill and a campaign, not a tariff; what it
does is make the government explain, every winter, why it has not adopted
one.?]

- @poland_event_queue: Attach the shield

@nuclear_supply_chain
title: Demand a Polish supply chain and one agreement covering the site
subtitle: Local content, apprenticeships and a single collective agreement instead of eight thousand subcontracted workers.
on-arrival: {!
Q.nuclear_labour_standard += 22;
Q.nuclear_left_credit += 5;
Q.nuclear_cost_pressure += 4;
Q.nuclear_position = "Polish supply chain and a single site agreement";
Q.labor_credibility += 4;
Q.workers_support += 3;
Q.public_sector_support += 1;
Q.razem_dissent -= 2;
Q.labor_dissent -= 2;
if (Q.left_in_government) {
    Q.nuclear_delivery += 2;
    Q.last_policy_stage = "Negotiated concession";
    Q.last_policy_authority = "Contract conditions agreed with the consortium and the state company";
    Q.last_policy_implementation = "Local content and a site collective agreement written into the contract";
} else {
    Q.last_policy_stage = "Proposal";
    Q.last_policy_authority = "Trade-union and opposition campaign";
    Q.last_policy_implementation = "Not enacted";
}
Q.news_headline = "The nuclear site is to be built under one collective agreement";
!}

An American consortium building a Polish plant with Korean, Turkish and Polish
subcontractors is a labour question before it is anything else. So the demand is
specific: a defined share of the work to Polish firms, a training pipeline
through the technical schools in Pomerania and Silesia, and one agreement
covering everybody on the site regardless of whose logo is on the helmet.

Westinghouse's negotiators have seen the requirement before and price it without
drama. The resistance comes from the Polish subcontracting sector, which built
its margins on exactly the arrangement being outlawed here. The metalworkers'
and construction unions get their first look at a Polish industrial project
where the union office exists before the first concrete does.

- @poland_event_queue: Write the site agreement

@nuclear_grid_first
title: Insist the grid, storage and wind come first
subtitle: A reactor that arrives in the 2030s cannot heat a flat this winter; put the money into the network that can.
on-arrival: {!
Q.nuclear_priority -= 15;
Q.nuclear_left_credit += 3;
Q.nuclear_position = "Grid, storage and offshore wind before further nuclear commitments";
Q.energy_security += 3;
Q.urban_progressive_support += 2;
Q.young_support += 2;
if (Q.left_in_government) {
    Q.climate_state_capacity += 4;
    Q.last_policy_stage = "Implementation";
    Q.last_policy_authority = "Energy ministry investment programme";
    Q.last_policy_implementation = "Grid, storage and offshore programmes funded ahead of further nuclear stages";
} else {
    Q.last_policy_stage = "Proposal";
    Q.last_policy_authority = "Opposition energy programme";
    Q.last_policy_implementation = "Not enacted";
}
if ((Number(Q.matysiak_nuclear_program) || 0) >= 5) {
    Q.razem_dissent += 4;
}
Q.news_headline = "Lewica puts the grid and offshore wind ahead of the next reactor";
!}

The case is arithmetical rather than ideological. The first reactor produces
nothing until the middle of the next decade. The distribution network is already
refusing connections, the offshore wind farms have nowhere to send their output
after 2029, and storage is the cheapest capacity anybody can build in four
years.

So the position is sequencing, not opposition: finish Lubiatowo, connect it, and
stop signing letters of intent for reactors nobody has financed. [? if
matysiak_nuclear_program >= 5: The industrial wing of our own coalition treats
this as the old anti-nuclear argument in a costume, and says so at a press
conference we did not schedule.?] The energy regulator, which has been asking
for network money for a decade, is quietly the biggest winner.

- @poland_event_queue: Fund the network

@nuclear_opposition_backing
title: Back the programme in public and name our price later
subtitle: Refuse PiS the story that the atom dies without them, and bank the credit for the financing fight.
view-if: left_in_government = 0
on-arrival: {!
Q.nuclear_priority += 10;
Q.nuclear_left_credit += 4;
Q.nuclear_position = "Cross-party support offered, conditions reserved for the financing vote";
Q.public_trust += 2;
Q.workers_support += 2;
Q.pis_relation += 2;
Q.economic_issue_salience += 2;
Q.progressives_dissent += 2;
Q.last_policy_stage = "Proposal";
Q.last_policy_authority = "Opposition public position";
Q.last_policy_implementation = "Not enacted";
Q.news_headline = "Lewica guarantees the nuclear programme cross-party support";
!}

We say the useful thing plainly: the plant should be built, the contract should
be honoured, and no future government of ours will cancel it. It costs nothing
and it removes, at a stroke, the most effective line the right has about the
Left and industry.

Our green wing dislikes the timing and dislikes the absence of conditions more.
The answer they get in the caucus room is not inspiring but is true: the
conditions are worth more attached to the financing statute than to a press
release, and the financing statute is a year away. Between now and then, the
promise is the asset.

- @poland_event_queue: Give the guarantee

@nuclear_financing_2025
# prose-audit: Grade A — The capital injection, the contract for difference, the Commission notification and the presidential signature are kept as separate gates; full public financing, shared capital, a conditioned package, an opposition ownership demand and a Left-led shelving each carry their own cost.
title: Sixty billion złoty and a signature
subtitle: The capital injection and the price contract have to survive the Sejm, the Commission and the Palace
new-page: true
tags: poland_event
priority: 26
order: 14
max-visits: 1
face-image: img/poland/events/lubiatowo-kopalino-2025.webp
view-if: continuous_campaign = 1 and nuclear_stage = 1 and ((year = 2025 and month >= 9) or year >= 2026)
on-arrival: {!
Q.nuclear_stage = 2;
Q.news_headline = "The nuclear financing package reaches the Sejm";
!}

= [+ date_label +] — The largest single line in the budget

The package is finally written. A direct capital injection into Polskie
Elektrownie Jądrowe of roughly sixty billion złoty, a contract for difference
guaranteeing the plant's price for decades, and a notification to the **European
Commission**, because state aid on this scale is not a domestic matter.

Three gates, in order: the Sejm's arithmetic, the Commission's competition
lawyers, and the President's pen. Any of them can cost a year.

[? if nuclear_public_ownership >= 20: The ownership statute we passed holds, so
this is public money going into an asset that stays public. That makes the aid
case in Brussels harder and the case at home easier.?]
[? if nuclear_price_shield >= 15: The household shield is attached to the
package, which means the price contract has to be drafted around it rather than
over it.?]
[? if president_relation < 25: The Palace has said nothing helpful. A statute of
this size arriving on a hostile desk is a real risk, not a formality.?]
[? if kpo_funds_available >= 10: Recovery-fund money is available for the grid
component, which changes what the Commission will tolerate elsewhere in the
package.?]

- @nuclear_finance_public
- @nuclear_finance_shared
- @nuclear_finance_conditioned
- @nuclear_finance_opposition
- @nuclear_finance_shelve

@nuclear_finance_public
title: Finance it entirely from the state and defend the number
subtitle: Sixty billion of public capital, no equity partner, and an argument about the deficit in every budget until 2036.
view-if: left_in_government = 1
on-arrival: {!
Q.nuclear_public_ownership += 15;
Q.nuclear_cost_pressure += 18;
Q.nuclear_delivery += 6;
Q.nuclear_left_credit += 4;
Q.nuclear_financing_model = "Direct state capital injection with no equity partner";
Q.government_delivery += 3;
Q.energy_security += 3;
Q.economic_issue_salience += 3;
Q.ko_relation -= 2;
Q.psl_relation -= 1;
Q.last_policy_stage = "Passage";
Q.last_policy_authority = "Sejm statute, European Commission state-aid clearance and presidential signature";
Q.last_policy_implementation = "Capital injection and price contract enacted in full public ownership";
Q.news_headline = "The Sejm votes sixty billion złoty of public capital into the atom";
!}

The bill passes with a majority that includes people who will campaign against
the deficit it creates. Brussels takes seven months and clears it with
conditions about the price contract that the ministry can live with.

What follows is the political weather for the rest of the decade: every hospital
waiting list, every teachers' pay round and every housing programme will now be
answered by somebody holding up the nuclear line in the budget. We knew that when
we voted. The counter-argument has to be made in public, repeatedly, for years —
that a country which cannot generate its own electricity has no social policy at
all, only a series of emergency tariffs.

- @poland_event_queue: Pass the package

@nuclear_finance_shared
title: Share the capital with an outside investor
subtitle: Bring in an export-credit and equity partner, halve the budget exposure, and give a foreign shareholder a permanent seat.
view-if: left_in_government = 1
on-arrival: {!
Q.nuclear_public_ownership -= 15;
Q.nuclear_cost_pressure -= 14;
Q.nuclear_delivery += 7;
Q.nuclear_left_credit -= 3;
Q.nuclear_financing_model = "Shared capital with export credit and a foreign equity partner";
Q.government_delivery += 3;
Q.us_alliance_reliability += 3;
Q.ko_relation += 3;
Q.razem_dissent += 5;
Q.labor_dissent += 2;
Q.progressives_dissent += 1;
Q.workers_support -= 1;
Q.last_policy_stage = "Negotiated concession";
Q.last_policy_authority = "Council of Ministers, export credit agencies and the state company board";
Q.last_policy_implementation = "Shared financing agreed; minority equity conceded";
Q.news_headline = "Export credit and a foreign shareholder cut the state's nuclear bill";
!}

Export credit from the supplier's government, a minority equity holding, and a
budget exposure roughly half the size of the alternative. The schedule improves
because the money arrives without waiting for a Polish budget cycle.

The shareholder agreement is where the argument lives. A minority partner with
veto rights over the price contract is not a passive investor, and the clause
that says so is on page four hundred. Razem publishes the comparison with our
2024 ownership condition within a day. What we bought is a plant that gets built;
what we sold is the sentence we used to say about who would own it.

- @poland_event_queue: Sign the financing agreement

@nuclear_finance_conditioned
title: Pass it with the grid, the shield and the just-transition package attached
subtitle: Cost: 2 resources. One statute: the reactor, the network, the household tariff and the coal regions, or none of it.
choose-if: resources >= 2
unavailable-subtitle: The party cannot hold a four-part package together this month.
on-arrival: {!
Q.resources -= 2;
Q.nuclear_price_shield += 15;
Q.nuclear_cost_pressure += 12;
Q.nuclear_left_credit += 8;
Q.nuclear_just_transition += 20;
Q.nuclear_financing_model = "Nuclear, grid, household shield and just transition in one statute";
Q.energy_security += 3;
Q.household_security += 3;
Q.workers_support += 4;
Q.labor_credibility += 4;
Q.social_spending_support += 2;
Q.razem_dissent -= 4;
if (Q.left_in_government) {
    Q.climate_state_capacity += 4;
    Q.nuclear_delivery += 4;
    Q.ko_relation -= 3;
    Q.government_coalition_dissent += 2;
    Q.last_policy_stage = "Passage";
    Q.last_policy_authority = "Sejm statute negotiated inside the coalition";
    Q.last_policy_implementation = "Combined energy, tariff and transition package enacted";
} else {
    Q.last_policy_stage = "Proposal";
    Q.last_policy_authority = "Opposition amendment package";
    Q.last_policy_implementation = "Not enacted";
}
Q.news_headline = "The atom, the grid, the tariff and the coal regions arrive as one bill";
!}

Four things in one statute, deliberately impossible to separate: the capital for
Lubiatowo, a network and storage programme, the household tariff shield, and
funded retraining and pensions for Bełchatów, Konin and the Silesian mines that
close before the reactor opens.

The drafting is a nightmare and the politics are the reason it works. A deputy
who wants the reactor has to vote for the miners' package; a deputy who wants the
miners' package has to vote for the reactor. [? if left_in_government = 1: Our
coalition partners spend three weeks trying to split it and then vote for
it.?][? if left_in_government = 0: The government splits it, takes the reactor
and leaves the rest, and every union in the country now knows precisely who
removed which clause.?]

- @poland_event_queue: Table the package

@nuclear_finance_opposition
title: Vote for the money and force a vote on the ownership
subtitle: Support the plant, then make the chamber say on the record whether it can ever be sold.
view-if: left_in_government = 0
on-arrival: {!
Q.nuclear_left_credit += 4;
Q.nuclear_ownership_rollcall = 1;
Q.public_trust += 2;
Q.workers_support += 2;
Q.economic_issue_salience += 2;
Q.ko_relation -= 2;
Q.last_policy_stage = "Passage";
Q.last_policy_authority = "Sejm roll call on a Lewica amendment";
Q.last_policy_implementation = "Financing enacted; ownership amendment recorded and defeated";
Q.news_headline = "Lewica votes the nuclear money through and loses the ownership clause on the record";
!}

We vote for the package, because an opposition that blocks the country's
electricity supply to make a point about ownership deserves what happens next.
Then we table the amendment we actually care about: no share of the company may
be sold without a statute.

It is defeated. The list of names is the product. It goes into the file with the
CPK roll call and the tariff votes, and it will be reprinted the first time a
minister discusses "bringing in strategic investors". Opposition politics is
largely the business of making future decisions attributable in advance.

- @poland_event_queue: Table the amendment

@nuclear_finance_shelve
title: Shelve the programme and rebuild the energy plan around the grid
subtitle: Only a Left-led cabinet can carry this. It saves sixty billion, breaks a state contract and hands the right a decade of accusation.
view-if: left_in_government = 1 and government_party = "lewica"
on-arrival: {!
Q.nuclear_shelved = 1;
Q.nuclear_priority -= 30;
Q.nuclear_cost_pressure -= 25;
Q.nuclear_delivery -= 6;
Q.nuclear_left_credit += 2;
Q.nuclear_financing_model = "Programme shelved; capital redirected to network, storage and renewables";
Q.climate_state_capacity += 6;
Q.energy_security -= 6;
Q.household_policy_pressure += 2;
Q.urban_progressive_support += 3;
Q.young_support += 2;
Q.workers_support -= 3;
Q.us_alliance_reliability -= 6;
Q.pis_relation -= 8;
Q.ko_relation -= 4;
if ((Number(Q.matysiak_nuclear_program) || 0) >= 5) {
    Q.razem_dissent += 6;
}
Q.last_policy_stage = "Implementation";
Q.last_policy_authority = "Council of Ministers under a Lewica prime minister";
Q.last_policy_implementation = "Nuclear programme suspended; capital redirected to network and renewables";
Q.news_headline = "The government suspends the nuclear programme and rewrites the energy plan";
!}

The decision is taken in one sitting and will be litigated for years. The
engineering contract is settled rather than torn up — the state pays for the work
done and the design it now owns — and the capital goes into the network, storage,
offshore wind and the heat systems of the cities that still burn coal.

The costs arrive immediately. Washington regards a cancelled American nuclear
contract as an alliance matter and says so at ambassadorial level. The energy
unions at Lubiatowo, who had been promised a generation of work, are not
consoled by a network programme in another voivodeship. And the right now has a
sentence it will use until 2040: they cancelled the airport, and then they
cancelled the power station.

- @poland_event_queue: Suspend the programme

@nuclear_second_plant_2026
# prose-audit: Grade B — The second-plant decision keeps the Korean partner, the small-reactor fleet and a grid-only answer as real alternatives with distinct coal-region consequences; the Konin and Bełchatów stakes are authored, though the foreign partners remain institutions rather than named negotiators.
title: The second plant, or something else entirely
subtitle: Konin wants a reactor, Orlen wants a small-reactor fleet, and the network wants the same money
new-page: true
tags: poland_event
priority: 24
order: 14
max-visits: 1
face-image: img/poland/events/lubiatowo-kopalino-2025.webp
view-if: continuous_campaign = 1 and nuclear_stage = 2 and nuclear_shelved = 0 and ((year = 2026 and month >= 1) or year >= 2027)
on-arrival: {!
Q.nuclear_stage = 3;
Q.news_headline = "The second nuclear project forces a choice between Konin, small reactors and the grid";
!}

= [+ date_label +] — Where the next one goes, if there is a next one

The first plant is under construction and the queue has formed behind it. The
Pątnów–Konin site, beside a lignite complex whose last unit closes this decade,
has a Korean partner that has been waiting since 2022 and a workforce that has
been waiting longer. Orlen's joint venture wants a fleet of small modular
reactors on industrial sites, financed largely by private capital. The grid
operator wants everybody to stop and look at the network map.

Konin is the political fact. Three thousand direct jobs end with the lignite,
in a region that voted for whoever promised to keep them, and a reactor is the
only proposal anybody has made that replaces them with the same kind of work.

[? if nuclear_just_transition >= 15: The transition package we attached to the
financing statute is already operating there, which means the retraining money
exists whatever is decided about the reactor.?]
[? if nuclear_public_ownership >= 25: Every option that involves private capital
now has to be argued against the ownership statute we passed.?]
[? if nuclear_labour_standard >= 20: The site agreement from Lubiatowo is the
template the unions will demand for whatever is built next.?]

- @nuclear_second_konin
- @nuclear_second_smr
- @nuclear_second_grid
- @nuclear_second_opposition

@nuclear_second_konin
title: Commit to Konin with the Korean partner and a public majority
subtitle: A second plant where the lignite closes, built under the same agreement, with the state holding control.
view-if: left_in_government = 1
