Q.last_policy_implementation = "Second plant committed at Pątnów–Konin under public majority control";
Q.news_headline = "The second reactor goes to Konin, where the lignite is closing";
!}

The agreement is signed with the state holding the majority, the Korean partner
supplying technology and construction capacity, and the Lubiatowo site agreement
extended to the new project before the first survey is done.

Konin understands what has been decided better than Warsaw does. The men who run
the lignite units are not sentimental about coal; they are protecting a wage, a
shift pattern and a town. Offering them the same thing with a different fuel is
the only just-transition policy that has ever worked anywhere. Washington's
displeasure at a Korean award is real and survivable; the alternative was
explaining to a region that its replacement industry is a training voucher.

- @poland_event_queue: Sign the Konin agreement

@nuclear_second_smr
title: Back the small-reactor fleet on industrial sites
subtitle: Faster, smaller, largely private — heat and power for industry, and a fleet the state does not own.
view-if: left_in_government = 1
on-arrival: {!
Q.nuclear_second_plant = "Private small modular reactor fleet on industrial sites";
Q.nuclear_public_ownership -= 12;
