} else {
    Q.last_policy_stage = "Proposal";
    Q.last_policy_authority = "Opposition energy programme";
    Q.last_policy_implementation = "Not enacted";
}
Q.workers_support -= 2;
if ((Number(Q.nuclear_just_transition) || 0) < 15) {
    Q.rural_support -= 2;
    Q.labor_dissent += 3;
}
Q.news_headline = "Poland stops at one nuclear programme and funds the network instead";
!}

The position is that a state which has never completed a reactor should finish
one before promising three more, and that the binding constraint on Polish
electricity this decade is wires, not generation.

It is the responsible answer and it is delivered to Konin by people who will not
be living there when the lignite units close. [? if nuclear_just_transition >=
15: The transition package at least means the money arrives regardless of the
reactor, which is the difference between a policy and an apology.?][? if
nuclear_just_transition < 15: There is no transition package to soften it, and
the region hears exactly one thing: no.?]

- @poland_event_queue: Fund the network

@nuclear_second_opposition
title: Make Konin the test of everybody's just-transition promises
subtitle: Take the region's case to the Sejm and force each party to say what replaces the lignite.
view-if: left_in_government = 0
on-arrival: {!
Q.nuclear_left_credit += 4;
Q.nuclear_just_transition += 8;
Q.labor_credibility += 4;
Q.workers_support += 3;
Q.rural_support += 2;
Q.local_network += 2;
Q.economic_issue_salience += 2;
Q.last_policy_stage = "Proposal";
Q.last_policy_authority = "Opposition parliamentary and trade-union campaign";
Q.last_policy_implementation = "Not enacted";
Q.news_headline = "Lewica makes Konin the test of every party's transition promise";
!}

We bring the works council, the mayor and the regional union to Warsaw and put
the question in a form nobody can answer with a slogan: the units close in this
decade, three thousand jobs go, what replaces them and on what date.

Nothing is decided, because we decide nothing. What changes is the register of
the argument. Until now the second plant has been discussed as an industrial
strategy question between ministries and investors; after this week it is
discussed as a question about a town, and every party has a recorded answer that
Konin can read back to it.

- @poland_event_queue: Bring the delegation

@nuclear_after_shelving_2026
# prose-audit: Grade B — The shelving branch is given a real aftermath: the abandoned coast, the alliance damage, the network programme that has to work, and the industrial wing that never accepted the decision.
title: The coast after the reactors were cancelled
subtitle: Choczewo has the site works, the compensation and no plant; the network programme now has to be worth it
new-page: true
tags: poland_event
priority: 24
order: 15
max-visits: 1
face-image: img/poland/events/lubiatowo-kopalino-2025.webp
view-if: continuous_campaign = 1 and nuclear_stage = 2 and nuclear_shelved = 1 and ((year = 2026 and month >= 1) or year >= 2027)
on-arrival: {!
Q.nuclear_stage = 3;
Q.news_headline = "The suspended nuclear programme leaves a prepared site and an unfinished argument";
!}

= [+ date_label +] — A prepared site and no reactor

Choczewo has a cleared site, a new road, a rebuilt substation, an access
railway under construction and no power station. The commune, which negotiated
for years over what it would receive for hosting the plant, is receiving it for
hosting a decision.

The network programme that replaced the reactors is real and is spending money:
transmission, storage, the first large heat pumps in the district systems of
three cities, and the offshore connections that had nowhere to go. None of it
photographs like a reactor.

[? if us_alliance_reliability < 40: The American side has not moved on. The
cancelled contract appears in every conversation about defence procurement,
usually in the first ten minutes.?]
[? if matysiak_nuclear_program >= 5: Our own industrial wing has not accepted
the decision either, and has stopped pretending otherwise in public.?]
[? if left_in_government = 1: We are still the government, so the network
programme has to work, visibly, before the next election.?][? if
left_in_government = 0: We are no longer in the cabinet that suspended it,
which does not stop the decision from being ours in every campaign.?]

- @nuclear_shelved_deliver
- @nuclear_shelved_coast
- @nuclear_shelved_reopen
- @nuclear_shelved_defend

@nuclear_shelved_deliver
title: Make the network programme visible before the election
subtitle: Cost: 2 resources. Connections, storage and heat in named towns, with dates, published against the cancelled reactor.
choose-if: resources >= 2
unavailable-subtitle: The party cannot carry a delivery campaign of this size this month.
on-arrival: {!
Q.resources -= 2;
Q.nuclear_left_credit += 6;
Q.economic_issue_salience += 2;
if (Q.left_in_government) {
    Q.energy_security += 4;
    Q.household_security += 2;
    Q.climate_state_capacity += 5;
    Q.government_delivery += 2;
    Q.last_policy_stage = "Implementation";
    Q.last_policy_authority = "Energy ministry and the transmission operator";
    Q.last_policy_implementation = "Network, storage and heat programme delivered against a published schedule";
} else {
    Q.last_policy_stage = "Proposal";
    Q.last_policy_authority = "Opposition delivery audit";
    Q.last_policy_implementation = "Not enacted";
}
Q.news_headline = "The energy programme that replaced the reactors publishes its dates";
!}

Every substation, every storage site, every heat system, with the town, the
contractor and the month it goes live. Anybody can check it, which is the only
reason it means anything.

Two things happen. Industrial customers who spent a year saying the country was
losing its nuclear future start reporting connection offers they had been told
were impossible until 2032. And the argument about the cancellation stops being a
question of belief and becomes a comparison between a reactor that would have
produced power in the middle of the next decade and a network that produces it
in this one. We do not win that argument everywhere. We win it where the
connections arrive.

- @poland_event_queue: Publish the schedule

@nuclear_shelved_coast
title: Give Choczewo the industry it was promised
subtitle: Turn the prepared site into an offshore wind and grid manufacturing base, with the same wage agreement.
view-if: left_in_government = 1
on-arrival: {!
Q.nuclear_left_credit += 5;
Q.nuclear_labour_standard += 12;
Q.nuclear_just_transition += 15;
Q.workers_support += 3;
Q.labor_credibility += 3;
Q.rural_support += 3;
Q.local_network += 2;
Q.energy_security += 2;
Q.climate_state_capacity += 3;
Q.last_policy_stage = "Implementation";
Q.last_policy_authority = "Council of Ministers industrial programme and the state energy companies";
Q.last_policy_implementation = "Offshore and grid manufacturing base sited on the prepared coastal site";
Q.news_headline = "The abandoned nuclear site becomes an offshore manufacturing base";
!}

The site has a deep-water approach, a rebuilt grid connection, a rail spur and
planning consent for heavy industry. Those are the conditions for exactly one
other thing: building the components of the offshore wind farms that Poland is
currently importing from Denmark and Germany.

The commune is not sentimental. It wanted a payroll and a rateable value, and it
is offered a payroll and a rateable value, under the same wage agreement the
nuclear consortium would have signed. The Kashubian fishermen who opposed the
plant are less pleased about a factory than they were about a cancellation, and
say so at the consultation. Nothing about this coast is going to be quiet again.

- @poland_event_queue: Site the factory

@nuclear_shelved_reopen
title: Reopen the file and admit the suspension was wrong
subtitle: Restart the programme on new terms, pay the price of the reversal, and rebuild what the decision cost.
view-if: left_in_government = 1
on-arrival: {!
Q.nuclear_shelved = 0;
Q.nuclear_priority += 20;
Q.nuclear_cost_pressure += 20;
Q.nuclear_delivery -= 2;
Q.nuclear_left_credit -= 5;
Q.nuclear_financing_model = "Programme restarted after suspension, on renegotiated terms";
Q.us_alliance_reliability += 5;
Q.energy_security += 2;
Q.workers_support += 2;
Q.public_trust -= 4;
Q.party_unity -= 4;
Q.urban_progressive_support -= 2;
Q.last_policy_stage = "Implementation";
Q.last_policy_authority = "Council of Ministers reversal resolution";
Q.last_policy_implementation = "Nuclear programme restarted on renegotiated terms";
Q.news_headline = "The government restarts the nuclear programme it suspended";
!}

The reversal is announced without theatre, which does not help. A government
that cancels a power station and restarts it two years later has told the
country something about itself that no press conference can un-tell, and the
restarted programme costs more than the original because everybody now prices
Polish political risk into the quote.

What it buys is real: the coast gets the plant, the alliance conversation
becomes survivable, and the industrial wing of our own coalition stops treating
the leadership as an enemy. What it costs is the assumption that our decisions
are decisions.

- @poland_event_queue: Restart the programme

@nuclear_shelved_defend
title: Defend the suspension on its own terms
subtitle: Argue the arithmetic in public every time it is raised, and accept a permanent line of attack.
on-arrival: {!
Q.nuclear_left_credit += 2;
Q.urban_progressive_support += 2;
Q.young_support += 2;
Q.workers_support -= 2;
Q.economic_issue_salience += 2;
if ((Number(Q.matysiak_nuclear_program) || 0) >= 5) {
    Q.razem_dissent += 3;
}
Q.last_policy_stage = "Proposal";
Q.last_policy_authority = "Party public argument";
Q.last_policy_implementation = "Not enacted";
Q.news_headline = "Lewica defends the nuclear suspension in public rather than avoiding it";
!}

We take the argument rather than the silence: the timetable, the cost per
megawatt, the network constraint, the projects that were funded instead. It is
made in television studios by people who have memorised the figures and are
prepared to be shouted at.

Roughly a third of the country finds it convincing, which is more than nothing
and less than a majority. The rest hear a party explaining why it did not build
something. This is the settled cost of the decision, and there is no version of
the next campaign in which we do not pay it.

- @poland_event_queue: Make the argument

@nuclear_site_2026
# prose-audit: Grade A — The construction phase is written as a labour, housing and community question on the Baltic coast; the wage inspection, the host compensation, the schedule-first route and the opposition organising route each land on named constituencies.
title: Eight thousand people arrive on the Baltic coast
subtitle: The largest construction workforce in the country reaches a commune of five thousand
new-page: true
tags: poland_event
priority: 24
order: 16
max-visits: 1
face-image: img/poland/events/lubiatowo-kopalino-2025.webp
view-if: continuous_campaign = 1 and nuclear_stage = 3 and nuclear_shelved = 0 and ((year = 2026 and month >= 8) or year >= 2027)
on-arrival: {!
Q.nuclear_stage = 4;
Q.news_headline = "The nuclear site becomes the country's largest workplace";
!}

= [+ date_label +] — A workforce larger than the commune

Excavation is finished and the main construction phase has begun. The workforce
on the Lubiatowo site passes eight thousand people in a commune of about five
thousand residents. Some are Polish, most are not; the chains of subcontracting
run through four countries; and the accommodation villages have gone up faster
than anything the local council has ever permitted.

The list of complaints is entirely predictable and entirely real. Rents in
Wejherowo and Lębork. A road system built for a summer tourist season carrying
concrete lorries in February. Fishermen with a construction exclusion zone in
their grounds. And the wage question underneath all of it: what a Turkish
welder, a Polish electrician and a Filipino scaffolder are paid for the same
shift on the same site.

[? if nuclear_labour_standard >= 20: The site agreement we negotiated covers all
of it on paper. Whether it covers it in practice depends on who is standing in
the accommodation village at six in the morning.?][? if nuclear_labour_standard <
20: There is no site agreement. Each contractor sets its own terms, and the
differences are known to everybody on the site within a week.?]
[? if left_in_government = 1: The State Labour Inspection answers to a ministry
in this cabinet, which makes enforcement a decision rather than a
complaint.?][? if left_in_government = 0: We have no ministry here. We have
organisers, lawyers and the ability to make a story out of a payslip.?]

- @nuclear_site_inspection
- @nuclear_site_community
- @nuclear_site_schedule
- @nuclear_site_organise

@nuclear_site_inspection
title: Put a labour inspectorate office on the site itself
subtitle: Permanent inspectors, interpreters, one pay rate for one job, and the power to stop a subcontractor.
view-if: left_in_government = 1
on-arrival: {!
Q.nuclear_labour_standard += 20;
Q.nuclear_left_credit += 6;
Q.nuclear_cost_pressure += 4;
Q.nuclear_delivery -= 1;
Q.labor_credibility += 5;
Q.workers_support += 4;
Q.public_sector_support += 2;
Q.household_security += 1;
Q.razem_dissent -= 2;
Q.last_policy_stage = "Implementation";
Q.last_policy_authority = "State Labour Inspection and the labour ministry";
Q.last_policy_implementation = "Permanent on-site inspection with interpreters and stop-work powers";
Q.news_headline = "Labour inspectors move onto the nuclear site permanently";
!}

Not a visiting team: an office, with inspectors who live locally, interpreters
in four languages, and the authority to stop a subcontractor's work rather than
write to its head office in another country.

The first three months produce a hundred and forty rectifications and two
suspensions, one of them of a firm that had been assured this was theatre. Word
travels through the accommodation villages faster than through the ministry. By
autumn the site has the highest rate of union membership of any construction
project in Poland, and the consortium's own schedulers admit privately that the
turnover rate has fallen enough to pay for the disruption.

- @poland_event_queue: Open the inspection office

@nuclear_site_community
title: Pay the coast for what it is carrying
subtitle: Host compensation, housing, the road programme and a settlement with the fishermen.
on-arrival: {!
Q.nuclear_site_conflict = Math.max(0, (Number(Q.nuclear_site_conflict) || 0) - 18);
Q.nuclear_left_credit += 5;
Q.nuclear_cost_pressure += 6;
Q.local_network += 3;
Q.rural_support += 3;
Q.public_trust += 2;
if (Q.left_in_government) {
    Q.household_security += 2;
    Q.last_policy_stage = "Implementation";
    Q.last_policy_authority = "Council of Ministers host-community regulation";
    Q.last_policy_implementation = "Host compensation, housing and fisheries settlement in force";
} else {
    Q.last_policy_stage = "Proposal";
    Q.last_policy_authority = "Opposition bill and local negotiation";
    Q.last_policy_implementation = "Not enacted";
}
Q.news_headline = "The coast is paid for hosting the reactor";
!}

A host payment to the commune that does not depend on a minister's goodwill,
municipal housing built for the people priced out of Wejherowo rather than for
the workforce, a road programme paid by the project, and a compensation
mechanism for the fishing families whose grounds are inside the exclusion zone.

The fishermen's association negotiates hard and settles, which surprises the
consortium and nobody who has ever negotiated with a fishermen's association.
The housing is the part that lasts: two hundred municipal flats on the coast,
still there when the site closes, in a country that has not built public housing
at that rate in a generation.

- @poland_event_queue: Sign the community package

@nuclear_site_schedule
title: Protect the schedule and settle the complaints later
subtitle: Keep the critical path, accept the subcontracting chain as it is, and hold the 2030s date.
view-if: left_in_government = 1
on-arrival: {!
Q.nuclear_delivery += 7;
Q.nuclear_site_conflict += 18;
Q.nuclear_labour_standard -= 10;
Q.nuclear_left_credit -= 6;
Q.government_delivery += 3;
Q.energy_security += 2;
Q.workers_support -= 3;
Q.labor_credibility -= 4;
Q.labor_dissent += 4;
Q.razem_dissent += 3;
Q.ko_relation += 2;
Q.last_policy_stage = "Implementation";
Q.last_policy_authority = "Council of Ministers and the state company board";
Q.last_policy_implementation = "Construction schedule prioritised over site labour and community measures";
Q.news_headline = "The government protects the nuclear schedule and defers the site complaints";
!}

The instruction to the company is to hold the date. Inspections are scheduled
around the concrete pours, the accommodation question is referred to the
commune, and the pay differentials are described as a matter for the
contractors.

The date holds, for now. So does everything else: the differentials, the rents,
the exclusion zone. In November a subcontractor's crew stops work over four
months of unpaid overtime, and the story runs for a week with our labour
minister's name attached to a project where the inspectorate visits by
appointment. We have delivered a construction milestone and handed away the
reason we were trusted with it.

- @poland_event_queue: Hold the date

@nuclear_site_organise
title: Organise the site from outside the government
subtitle: Cost: 1 resource. Union organisers, translated contracts and a legal line for anybody on the site who asks.
view-if: left_in_government = 0
choose-if: resources >= 1
unavailable-subtitle: The organising fund is empty.
on-arrival: {!
Q.resources -= 1;
Q.nuclear_labour_standard += 10;
Q.nuclear_left_credit += 5;
Q.labor_credibility += 5;
Q.workers_support += 4;
Q.local_network += 3;
Q.public_trust += 1;
Q.razem_dissent -= 2;
Q.last_policy_stage = "Proposal";
Q.last_policy_authority = "Trade-union organising and party legal support";
Q.last_policy_implementation = "Not enacted";
Q.news_headline = "Organisers reach the nuclear site's subcontracted workforce";
!}

Four organisers, a translated contract template, a legal helpline and a caravan
outside the accommodation village. It is nineteenth-century work performed with
a messaging app, and it is the only instrument an opposition has on a site like
this.

The first case that reaches a court is small: unpaid overtime for a crew of
eleven. It is won, publicly, and the rate for that shift changes across the whole
site within a month, because a contractor that has lost once prices the second
loss into its schedule. Membership follows the judgment rather than the leaflet,
and the union that arrives here will still be here when the government changes.

- @poland_event_queue: Send the organisers

@nuclear_price_2027
# prose-audit: Grade B — The scenario-horizon settlement reads ownership, tariffs, jobs and delivery back as an electricity-bill argument before the election; four Lewica endings are authored, though the closing beat relies on the accumulated ledger rather than new actors.
title: What the atom is worth on a bill — scenario horizon
subtitle: The reactors produce nothing yet, and the electorate is being asked to pay for them anyway
new-page: true
tags: poland_event
priority: 25
order: 15
max-visits: 1
face-image: img/poland/events/lubiatowo-kopalino-2025.webp
view-if: continuous_campaign = 1 and nuclear_stage >= 3 and nuclear_stage < 5 and ((year = 2027 and month >= 4) or year > 2027)
on-arrival: {!
Q.nuclear_stage = 5;
const publicHeld = (Number(Q.nuclear_public_ownership) || 0) >= 20;
const shielded = (Number(Q.nuclear_price_shield) || 0) >= 15;
Q.nuclear_settlement = Q.nuclear_shelved
    ? "A suspended nuclear programme and a network built instead of it"
    : (publicHeld
        ? "A publicly owned nuclear programme under construction"
        : "A nuclear programme with private and foreign shareholders");
Q.nuclear_bill_verdict = shielded
    ? "The household shield holds, and the bills have not carried the construction"
    : "The construction is being recovered through the tariff, and everybody can see it";
Q.news_headline = "Electricity prices become the campaign's economic argument";
!}

= [+ date_label +] — Scenario horizon: the bill arrives before the power does

**[+ nuclear_settlement +].** [+ nuclear_bill_verdict +].

[? if nuclear_delivery >= 15: The site is visibly a power station rather than an
excavation, which is worth more in a campaign than any figure.?][? if
nuclear_delivery < 15: There is nothing to open, nothing to switch on and
nothing to photograph except a very large building site.?]
[? if nuclear_labour_standard >= 25: Eight thousand people work there under one
agreement, and the union that negotiated it is ours in every way that
matters.?]
[? if nuclear_just_transition >= 20: In Konin and Bełchatów the transition money
is arriving in wages rather than in strategy documents, which is why the
lignite regions are no longer the safest ground the right owns.?]
[? if nuclear_cost_pressure >= 30: The cost of the programme is the opposition's
first slide at every press conference, and the figure is not wrong.?]

The last decision available before the election is what we say about it.

- @nuclear_final_tariff
- @nuclear_final_ownership
- @nuclear_final_jobs
- @nuclear_final_quiet

@nuclear_final_tariff
title: Campaign on the bill
subtitle: Guarantee that no household pays for construction, and publish the formula.
on-arrival: {!
Q.nuclear_price_shield += 12;
Q.nuclear_left_credit += 5;
Q.household_security += 3;
Q.social_spending_support += 2;
Q.pensioner_support += 3;
Q.workers_support += 2;
Q.turnout_readiness += 2;
Q.economic_issue_salience += 3;
Q.last_policy_stage = Q.left_in_government ? "Implementation" : "Proposal";
Q.last_policy_authority = Q.left_in_government
    ? "Tariff regulation and the energy ministry"
    : "Election programme of the Left";
Q.last_policy_implementation = Q.left_in_government
    ? "Construction costs excluded from household tariffs by regulation"
    : "Not enacted";
Q.news_headline = "Lewica campaigns on a bill that excludes the cost of construction";
!}

The commitment is narrow enough to be credible: the capital cost of the
programme does not enter household tariffs, the social tariff is indexed by a
published formula, and the regulator has to explain any deviation to the Sejm.

It is an argument about a piece of paper that arrives every two months, which
makes it the most concrete thing on our platform. Our opponents attack the
mechanism as unaffordable and are asked, on air, what happens to the bill under
their version. Nobody in this campaign has a good answer to that question except
us.

- @poland_event_queue: Publish the guarantee

@nuclear_final_ownership
title: Campaign on who owns it
subtitle: Make the sale of the programme a constitutional-scale promise, and force the other parties to match or refuse.
on-arrival: {!
Q.nuclear_public_ownership += 10;
Q.nuclear_left_credit += 4;
Q.public_trust += 3;
Q.workers_support += 3;
Q.economic_issue_salience += 2;
Q.ko_relation -= 3;
Q.market_liberal_support -= 2;
Q.last_policy_stage = Q.left_in_government ? "Passage" : "Proposal";
Q.last_policy_authority = Q.left_in_government
    ? "Sejm statute protecting public ownership"
    : "Election programme of the Left";
Q.last_policy_implementation = Q.left_in_government
    ? "Statutory protection of public ownership enacted"
    : "Not enacted";
Q.news_headline = "The Left makes public ownership of the atom an election promise";
!}

One sentence, repeated without variation: the country paid for it, the country
keeps it. No share sold, no operating concession, no clever structure involving
a fund registered somewhere warm.

[? if nuclear_ownership_rollcall = 1: The roll call from the financing statute
does the work for us. Every deputy who voted against the ownership clause is
listed by constituency, and the list is not a leaflet we wrote — it is the
Sejm's own record.?] The liberal centre finds itself defending flexibility about
an asset most voters believe they have already paid for, which is not a position
anybody chooses.

- @poland_event_queue: Make the promise

@nuclear_final_jobs
title: Campaign on the industrial settlement
subtitle: Cost: 1 resource. The site agreement, the supply chain and the transition regions as one continuing programme.
choose-if: resources >= 1
unavailable-subtitle: The industrial programme cannot be costed and printed this month.
on-arrival: {!
Q.resources -= 1;
Q.nuclear_left_credit += 6;
Q.nuclear_just_transition += 10;
Q.workers_support += 4;
Q.labor_credibility += 4;
Q.rural_support += 2;
Q.public_sector_support += 1;
Q.razem_dissent -= 3;
Q.labor_dissent -= 3;
Q.last_policy_stage = Q.left_in_government ? "Implementation" : "Proposal";
Q.last_policy_authority = Q.left_in_government
    ? "Industry ministry programme and the state energy companies"
    : "Election programme of the Left";
Q.last_policy_implementation = Q.left_in_government
    ? "Industrial and transition programme continued beyond the construction phase"
    : "Not enacted";
Q.news_headline = "The Left runs on the industrial settlement built around the reactor";
!}

The argument is that the plant was never only a plant. It was a site agreement
that set a wage floor for an entire sector, a supply chain that gave Polish
engineering firms a decade of orders, and a transition programme that reached
regions the last thirty years wrote off.

We put all three on one page with the names of the towns. It is the closest
thing this party has produced to an industrial policy since 1989, and — unusually
for our platforms — most of it already exists and can be visited.

- @poland_event_queue: Print the settlement

@nuclear_final_quiet
title: Say as little as possible about energy
subtitle: Avoid a subject that divides our own coalition and let the larger parties own it.
on-arrival: {!
Q.nuclear_left_credit -= 4;
Q.economic_issue_salience -= 1;
Q.party_unity -= 2;
Q.razem_dissent += 2;
Q.labor_dissent += 2;
Q.workers_support -= 1;
Q.last_policy_stage = "Proposal";
Q.last_policy_authority = "Party campaign decision";
Q.last_policy_implementation = "Not enacted";
Q.news_headline = "Lewica keeps energy off its own campaign material";
!}

The reasoning is that our green wing and our industrial wing have spent three
years arguing about the atom and a campaign is not the place to continue it. The
material goes to print with housing, health and wages, and no page about
electricity.

The subject does not go away because we stopped printing it. It arrives at every
doorstep in the form of a bill, and the answer given there is whatever the two
larger parties have decided it is. Our own energy people, who built the site
agreement and the tariff mechanism, watch a campaign run without the only piece
of industrial policy this party has ever delivered.

- @poland_event_queue: Leave it off the page
